course: dictionary
lesson: 14764
title: Prospective: almost, but not yet
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word prospective means likely or expected to become something in the future. Read the words near it for clues. «The school welcomed prospective pupils on open day.» The children are not pupils yet. They may join the school later, so prospective means likely to become a pupil.
explain: «The prospective buyers walked round the house and asked about the garden.» The people have not bought the house yet. The word prospective tells you they might buy it in the future. Always ask: what word would fit here? Try future or possible in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the meaning.
explain: Prospective is a formal word. You will often see it in phrases like «prospective students», «prospective buyers» and «prospective parents». Each phrase tells you the person is not yet that thing, but may be soon.
fact: Prospective means likely or expected to be or become something in the future.
fact: Prospective is an adjective, so it describes a person or thing: «prospective parents».
fact: «Prospective pupils» are children who may join a school soon.
fact: In formal writing, prospective can mean going to happen or take effect in the future, as in «the prospective changes to the rules».
fact: A good check is to swap in future or possible and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «The school welcomed prospective pupils on open day.» What does prospective mean here? | Pupils who have left the school | Already at the school | *Likely to become pupils in the future | Very clever pupils
exercise: Mum says a prospective buyer is someone who might buy a house. Why does the word prospective matter? | It means the buyer is angry | It means the house is very old | *It shows the person is not yet a buyer | It shows the person already owns the house
exercise: Ella says prospective means happy. Is she right? | *No, it means likely to become something in the future | Yes, it means happy and excited | No, it means finished | Yes, it means very tall
test: «The prospective parents were shown the classrooms.» Who are the prospective parents? | People who are already teachers | People who work at the school | *People who may become parents in the future | People who have never met a child
test: Which sentence uses prospective correctly? | *The prospective winner will be told on Friday. | The prospective river flowed past the school. | I prospective my homework every night. | The prospective cake was eaten yesterday.
exam: In «the prospective buyers looked at the house», prospective tells us | the buyers are very rich | *the buyers are not yet owners but may buy it | the house was sold in the past | the buyers already own the house
